===============================================================================

			Allelic Diversity Analyzer -- example

===============================================================================

This directory holds the worked example from section 6.1 of the manual
(../ADZE_Manual.pdf). Questions, problems and bug reports belong on the issue
tracker: https://github.com/szpiech/ADZE/issues

Input files
-----------

small_data.stru		11 diploid individuals in 3 groupings, typed at 5
			loci, in STRUCTURE format. Five label columns
			precede the genotypes; column 5 names the grouping.
small_paramfile.txt	a version 1.0 parameter file for this data set, kept
			as a demonstration that such files still work

Running it
----------

The parameter file still works as it always did, and the command line can
override any of its keywords:

	adze small_paramfile.txt

None of the four dimensions the parameter file declares is required any more.
The same analysis without a parameter file is

	adze --data small_data.stru --group-col 5 --tolerance 0.2 \
	     --combinations --tuples-k 2 --out-prefix small \
	     --full-richness --full-private --full-tuples

except for MAX_G, which is then resolved from the data instead of declared as
8. The two runs agree wherever they overlap, and the private allelic richness
and pair files are identical, but the richness files are not: the default is
the largest g every grouping supports, which is 6 here because EUROPE has six
gene copies, whereas declaring MAX_G 8 additionally reports AMERICA and
EAST_ASIA at g = 7 and 8, where their own sample sizes reach. Nothing is
private at a g that some grouping cannot reach, so the private and pair
statistics stop at 6 either way.

The --tolerance 0.2 above is the paramfile's value, not a default: without it
the default of 0.1 applies, which on this data set drops the same two loci and
so gives the same result here. Version 1.0's default was 1 (keep every locus).

Add --dry-run to either command to see the detected layout, the per-grouping
sample sizes and the largest feasible MAX_G without computing anything.

The result files shipped beside the data were regenerated with this version,
in its default layout: tab-separated, with a header row, and NA where a
statistic is undefined at that g.

	adze small_paramfile.txt --legacy

writes version 1.0's layout instead -- no header, space separators, undefined
rows omitted. That output differs from 1.0's published files in one respect
only: the sweep starts at g = 1, so each file carries one extra row per
grouping or tuple. Dropping those rows reproduces 1.0's files byte for byte,
which is checked in test/regress.py for the whole synthetic matrix as well.

Expected output
---------------

The remaining files are the published results of this example, produced with
version 1.0. Version 2.0 reproduces the four numerical ones byte-for-byte:

small_r			allelic richness		(--out-richness)
small_p			private allelic richness	(--out-private)
small_c_2		private alleles of grouping pairs (--out-tuples)
small_p_deletedloci	the loci dropped by TOLERANCE 0.2

The three *_fulldata files carry the same statistics at every locus. They
differ from a current run in one word of their header line: the distributed
copies say AVG where the source says MEAN, so they were produced by a build
that predates that rename. small_r_summary echoes the parameters and the
timing of each phase, and so differs from run to run.

Section 5 of the manual documents all of these formats.
